dante 1.4.4 libdsocks: keep the program's epoll registrations when a socket
is replaced

Applies on top of the other dante-*.patch files.

libdsocks replaces the socket behind a program's descriptor with dup2(2):
for every non-blocking connect (the program gets a placeholder, and the
socket connected to the proxy is dup2()ed onto it when the negotiation
finishes), and with dante-ipv6-gateway.patch whenever an IPv4 socket has to
become IPv6 to reach the proxy (TCP and the UDP data socket). epoll(7)
registrations belong to the open file, not the descriptor number, so they
vanish with the old socket. Programs that use epoll (libevent, libuv, most
event loops on Linux) are then never told that a non-blocking connect
finished, or that UDP data arrived: proxied connections never complete and
received datagrams pile up unread. Programs using select(2) or poll(2)
pass the descriptor on every call and are not affected.

libdsocks now interposes epoll_ctl(2) (not socksified, only recorded;
exported via include/symbols_preload.txt) and
remembers each registration with the identity (st_dev/st_ino) of the file
registered. socks_dup2(), used wherever libdsocks replaces one of the
program's sockets, registers the new socket with the same epoll instances,
events and data. Only registrations of the file being replaced move, so a
stale entry for a closed and reused descriptor does not. Linux only.

Limitation: a registration the program made *before* a non-blocking
connect(2) stays attached to the original socket, which libdsocks keeps
for the negotiation; such programs may see events for it early. Event loops
normally register after connect() returns EINPROGRESS.

Test: test/epollcheck.c (via test/run.sh).

